[Music]  okay quick quiz i want you to guess what  this symbol is ready go  any guesses  come on time's running out  i'll give you a clue it's in the title  okay what you're looking at is the  federal aviation administration's  proposed symbol to identify verti ports  a place where electric vertical takeoff  and landing or ev toll aircraft could  take off and land see the faa is in the  process of drawing up some guidance over  how ev tiles could fit into urban areas  but where you might see this symbol in  the future could make or break some of  these ev toll companies and the  decisions being made could impact  everything from your future commute to  how clear the skies are around you so if  ev tolls take off  where are they going to land let's find  out  [Music]  okay first i want to show you why the  choice of landing sites could be more  important for these companies than how  fast they can fly come with me i'll  explain  [Music]  so currently when i head into work i  have a few options for how i get there  the quickest are taxi tube or express  train now they all travel at different  speeds stop at different frequencies and  take different routes but no matter  which mode i take it always takes me  around an hour to get into the office  and that's because of where the  departure sites are  the taxi may be slower than the express  train but it goes directly from door to  door the express train may move far  quicker and more directly but the  stations are miles from my home and  office and require additional travelling  at the start and end  this is super important for these ev  toll companies because if they're going  to dramatically cut travel times they're  going to have to be exactly where  customers need them to be or as the  companies themselves say having these  sites located where  people want to move to and from is is  very important to us these locations  have to be nearby where the nodes of  traffic exist so what options are they  exploring  well the first option is probably the  most obvious underutilized existing  airports see while america has some 5  000 airports a whopping 70 of air  traffic is handled by just 30 of them  joby and whisk were among two companies  that told me that they planned to  initially operate from airports as they  offered existing infrastructure designed  for aircraft and had air traffic control  what's more according to the faa some 95  of the us population lives within 30  miles of an airport i think that there's  the benefit to operating in airports  primarily as they're already accustomed  to operating aircraft that's great if  you're flying into town and need to get  to another airport but less so if you're  trying to get anywhere else within the  city so companies like archer and others  are also investing in developing landing  sites on inner city rooftops and if  you're thinking you've heard that before  you'd be right after all there are  already helipads on some buildings and  they could potentially be adapted to  land evital aircraft but existing  helipads present their own problems like  trying to figure out how charging  infrastructure or passenger terminals  could be integrated and that's what  we're working on right now so from an  access perspective we're working with  cities to understand you know the tempo  of operations the hours of operations to  ensure that we're integrating ourselves  and our operations into the city in a  manner that obviously provides as  minimal disruption as possible archery  is also exploring deploying landing  sites on the roofs of car parks they're  well located in cities the rooftops are  generally very underutilized and they're  generally built to handle large moving  loads the next option is to take things  a step further and build standalone  verti ports these custom-built evtel  stations would act as a hub where  aircraft could take off and land but  also park and charge the companies i  spoke to told me they'd likely be built  alongside existing transport hubs like  adding a bus station to a railway  terminus but they stressed that they'd  take longer to develop i think when we  talk about virtaports and all that  that's  that's a long lead time but one company  i spoke to had a very different idea of  where ev toll should be able to take off  and land from we  want to land at any flat surface that  can physically afford the vehicle so we  are really disbelievers in building  specialized voter ports that's sebastian  throne kitty hawk ceo and former leader  of google's self-driving car project and  according to him ev tolls should be able  to operate like ubers in the sky tap a  button and one comes to your door when  you put additional hubs in the middle  you lose some of the benefits because  now people have to change modalities  they might have to wait along the way  and so on that's not a sentiment that  many in the industry seem to share it's  hard to imagine a world in which you  know having these aircraft landing on  our neighborhood cul-de-sacs is  promoting a culture of safety wait wait  i just want to bring us back down to  earth for a moment because right now  none of these vehicles have carried  paying passengers and some of them may  never commercially take off but if they  do and this symbol becomes the new  landing pad where will we see it  well according to the faa's draft  guidance to begin with urban air  mobility will use existing helicopter  infrastructure but in the meantime these  companies are starting to snatch up roof  space in cities as they try and further  integrate into the urban environment so  keep your eyes open if you see this  symbol on the ground in the next few  years it could mean that ev toll  aircraft are finally taking off  hey where would verti ports make the  most sense for you let me know in the  comments below and if you're interested  in the future of how we get from a to b  then don't forget to subscribe see you  soon  [Music]

